    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d719">

                <pb facs="0992" n="978" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg978"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/7–2244: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-22T15:00:00+07:00">July 22, 1944—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 22—11 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1276. Apparently scope of authority of Chinese Supply Commission, Washington, not
                    yet fully settled here. Re your 965, July 17, 2 p.m. It has been set up
                    primarily to handle lend-lease. Opinion here is that it desires also to handle
                    post-war reconstruction projects. There is doubt in some quarters whether it is
                    capable of doing so. However, Kung<anchor n="27" xml:id="d719-f1"/> is believed
                    to have been given considerable discretion. Cabinet Ministers here are
                    telegraphing Kung for clarification and we understand Kung’s recommendation is
                    also being sought whether former China Defense Supply operations here should now
                    be taken over by Minister of War<anchor n="28" xml:id="d719-f2"/> or entrusted
                    to Central Trust. Embassy believes Kung will likely endeavor to concentrate
                    authority in Central Trust here and Chinese Supply Commission, Washington, and
                    will also endeavor to control official post-war reconstruction projects. We will
                    continue to follow the matter and report when situation is clarified.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d719-f1" n="27" xml:id="d719-f1-content">
                <p>H. H. Kung, Chinese Minister of Finance, on mission to the United States.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d719-f2" n="28" xml:id="d719-f2-content">
                <p>Gen. Ho Ying chin.</p>
            </note>
